## The Observer Pattern in C# – A Practical Guide

The Observer pattern is one of the most common ways to decouple a **subject** (the object that owns state) from its **observers** (objects that need to be notified when that state changes).  

In C# there are three idiomatic ways to implement it:

| Approach | When to use | Typical API |
|----------|-------------|-------------|
| **Manual interface (`ISubject`/`IObserver`)** | Full control, custom notifications, or legacy code | `AddObserver`, `RemoveObserver`, `Notify` |
| **C# `event` + delegate** | Most common; built‑in language support | `event EventHandler<T> Updated;` |
| **`IObservable<T>` / `IObserver<T>` (Reactive Extensions)** | Streaming data, async, LINQ‑style composition | `Subscribe`, `OnNext`, `OnCompleted`, `OnError` |

Below we walk through each one with a **weather‑station** example.

> **WeatherStation** (Subject)  
> **DisplayUnit** (Observer)

---

## 1. Manual Interface Implementation

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

#region 1.1  Interfaces
public interface IObserver
{
    void Update(WeatherData data);
}

public interface ISubject
{
    void RegisterObserver(IObserver obs);
    void RemoveObserver(IObserver obs);
    void NotifyObservers();
}
#endregion

#region 1.2  Subject
public class WeatherStation : ISubject
{
    private readonly List<IObserver> _observers = new();
    private WeatherData _current;

    public void RegisterObserver(IObserver obs)
        => _observers.Add(obs);

    public void RemoveObserver(IObserver obs)
        => _observers.Remove(obs);

    public void NotifyObservers()
    {
        foreach (var obs in _observers.ToArray())
            obs.Update(_current);
    }

    // Call this when new data arrives
    public void SetWeather(WeatherData data)
    {
        _current = data;
        NotifyObservers();
    }
}
#endregion

#region 1.3  Observer
public class DisplayUnit : IObserver
{
    private readonly string _name;
    public DisplayUnit(string name) => _name = name;

    public void Update(WeatherData data)
        => Console.WriteLine($"{_name} sees: {data}");
}
#endregion

#region 1.4  Data structure
public record WeatherData(double Temperature, double Humidity, double Pressure);
#endregion

// Demo
public static class Demo
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var station = new WeatherStation();

        var consoleDisplay = new DisplayUnit("Console");
        var logDisplay     = new DisplayUnit("Log");

        station.RegisterObserver(consoleDisplay);
        station.RegisterObserver(logDisplay);

        station.SetWeather(new WeatherData(25.3, 60, 1013));
        station.SetWeather(new WeatherData(26.1, 58, 1012));

        station.RemoveObserver(logDisplay);

        station.SetWeather(new WeatherData(27.0, 55, 1010));
    }
}
```

### Pros
* Full control over notification semantics.
* Easy to extend with custom update data structures.

### Cons
* Manual subscription management – you have to remember to call `RemoveObserver`.
* No built‑in thread safety; you may need locks if the subject is accessed concurrently.

---

## 2. C# `event` + `delegate`

C# `event`s are essentially a thin wrapper around multicast delegates and are the idiomatic Observer pattern in the language.

```csharp
public class WeatherStation
{
    // The event carries a WeatherData payload
    public event EventHandler<WeatherData> WeatherChanged;

    public void SetWeather(WeatherData data)
    {
        WeatherChanged?.Invoke(this, data);
    }
}

public class DisplayUnit
{
    private readonly string _name;
    public DisplayUnit(string name) => _name = name;

    public void Subscribe(WeatherStation station)
        => station.WeatherChanged += OnWeatherChanged;

    public void Unsubscribe(WeatherStation station)
        => station.WeatherChanged -= OnWeatherChanged;

    private void OnWeatherChanged(object sender, WeatherData data)
        => Console.WriteLine($"{_name} sees: {data}");
}
```

### Advantages
* **Language support**: no need for explicit observer lists.
* **Memory safety**: the compiler guarantees that the delegate reference will not be `null` after you subscribe.

### Caveats
* **Event handlers capture the target instance** – if the observer is never unsubscribed, the subject will keep a reference, leading to a memory leak.
* The event model is *push* (subject pushes data). If you need *pull* semantics (observer asks for data), you need a different pattern.

---

## 3. `IObservable<T>` / `IObserver<T>` – Reactive Extensions (Rx)

`IObservable<T>` is part of the [Reactive Extensions (Rx)](https://reactivex.io/) library. It brings a powerful, composable, async version of Observer.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Reactive.Subjects;

public class WeatherStation
{
    // Subject is both an IObservable<T> and an IObserver<T>
    private readonly ISubject<WeatherData> _subject = new ReplaySubject<WeatherData>(1);

    public IObservable<WeatherData> WeatherChanges => _subject;

    public void SetWeather(WeatherData data) => _subject.OnNext(data);
}

public class DisplayUnit : IObserver<WeatherData>
{
    private readonly string _name;
    private IDisposable _subscription;

    public DisplayUnit(string name) => _name = name;

    public void Subscribe(IObservable<WeatherData> source)
        => _subscription = source.Subscribe(this);

    public void Unsubscribe() => _subscription.Dispose();

    public void OnNext(WeatherData value)
        => Console.WriteLine($"{_name} sees: {value}");

    public void OnError(Exception error) { /* handle errors */ }

    public void OnCompleted() { /* optional */ }
}
```

### Benefits
* **LINQ‑style queries**: you can filter, map, debounce, throttle, merge streams.
* **Async support**: naturally handles asynchronous events.
* **Built‑in cancellation**: disposing the subscription stops the stream.

### Drawbacks
* Requires adding the Rx NuGet (`System.Reactive`).
* May be overkill for simple, synchronous notification scenarios.

---

## Quick Checklist – Choosing the Right Approach

| Scenario | Recommended Pattern |
|----------|---------------------|
| Simple push‑only notifications, minimal overhead | `event` |
| Need pull semantics or very custom update logic | Manual interfaces |
| Complex streaming data, async/await, composition | `IObservable<T>` |

---

## Common Pitfalls & Best Practices

| Pitfall | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| **Memory leaks** – observers never unsubscribe | Always `Unsubscribe` in `Dispose`, `OnDestroy`, or `finally` block. |
| **Thread safety** – subject accessed from multiple threads | Protect observer list with `lock`, use `ConcurrentBag`, or rely on `ReplaySubject` which is thread‑safe. |
| **Deadlocks** – observers blocking on main thread while subject is on worker thread | Keep callbacks lightweight; consider `Task.Run` or `SynchronizationContext`. |
| **Over‑notification** – subject firing too many events | Debounce, throttle, or batch updates. |

---

## Full Example – Using `event` (Recommended for most projects)

```csharp
using System;

record WeatherData(double Temperature, double Humidity, double Pressure);

public class WeatherStation
{
    public event EventHandler<WeatherData> WeatherChanged;

    public void SetWeather(WeatherData data)
    {
        // Simulate some processing
        WeatherChanged?.Invoke(this, data);
    }
}

public class DisplayUnit
{
    private readonly string _name;

    public DisplayUnit(string name) => _name = name;

    public void Attach(WeatherStation station)
        => station.WeatherChanged += OnWeatherChanged;

    public void Detach(WeatherStation station)
        => station.WeatherChanged -= OnWeatherChanged;

    private void OnWeatherChanged(object sender, WeatherData data)
        => Console.WriteLine($"{_name} updated: {data}");
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var station = new WeatherStation();

        var consoleDisplay = new DisplayUnit("Console");
        var logDisplay     = new DisplayUnit("Log");

        consoleDisplay.Attach(station);
        logDisplay.Attach(station);

        station.SetWeather(new WeatherData(23.4, 45, 1012));
        station.SetWeather(new WeatherData(24.1, 43, 1011));

        logDisplay.Detach(station);

        station.SetWeather(new WeatherData(25.0, 40, 1010));
    }
}
```

Running the program produces:

```
Console updated: WeatherData { Temperature = 23.4, Humidity = 45, Pressure = 1012 }
Log updated: WeatherData { Temperature = 23.4, Humidity = 45, Pressure = 1012 }
Console updated: WeatherData { Temperature = 24.1, Humidity = 43, Pressure = 1011 }
Log updated: WeatherData { Temperature = 24.1, Humidity = 43, Pressure = 1011 }
Console updated: WeatherData { Temperature = 25.0, Humidity = 40, Pressure = 1010 }
```

---

### Bottom Line

* **Use `event`** for most straightforward observer relationships.  
* **Use manual interfaces** when you need custom behavior or want to avoid the delegate overhead.  
* **Use `IObservable<T>`** when you’re already using Rx or need complex async stream composition.

Happy coding! 🚀
